vening  to both of you nice of you to join us  here on sky news on a friday night we're  going to start aren't we with the front  page of the times and that uh photograph  of vladimir putin there polly at this  bizarre spectacle the rally that he held  at that football stadium in moscow  it was bizarre um it sort of had a  sort of worldwide whistling wrestling  federation kind of vibe to it lots of  hype and cheering  uh and he stood on a what looked like a  wrestling arena or a boxing arena right  in the middle of the stadium and and  gave this speech  uh in celebration of of what they claim  is a denuncification process of  of ukraine liberating russian speakers  in ukraine of course none of which has  any basis in reality but is the story  that russian leaders have been  propagating through russian state media  for the last eight years since that  annexation of of crimea it was you know  it's i think horrifying to see the way  that ordinary russians have been  caught up in these lies which have been  pushed on them by the state so  relentlessly and the and the power that  has to  to shape the narrative i think it's  incredibly important that um you know  alternative media sources find ways  where and however they can to get in to  reach the russian people and explain the  the lies that are being pushed on them  by this leader um part of the story  there matt also says that a lot of the  people who were in attendance at this at  this rally  are allegedly  public sector employees who have coerced  into going  yeah that's right it's obviously very  hard to verify anything that russia does  at the moment but um yeah the reports  are around 200 000 people there but many  people uh who i think some reporters on  the ground spoke to were forced public  servants sports to go at the end of  their shift or during their shift i  enjoyed this quote on one man who said  that most people here don't support the  war um i'll go for a bit uh but so then  i'll then i'll head off um it did it did  see there was a very sort of mixed  reaction from the crowd many people were  seen  leaving before it did it finished and i  it's it's obviously very hard to verify  uh what russians really think but um  yeah i guess i guess 200 000 of um out  of a huge population russia has i mean  it's it's not obviously a fraction of  population but um how many people were  there actually supporting  putin  no one knows and what i found it mean  it's quite sickening really the the  music that was played  one was headlined um made in the usa  sorry made in the ussr with the opening  line ukraine and crimea belarus and  moldova it's all my country um so i  think maybe it's vladimir putin trying  to rally his own country in the  knowledge that uh the war is uh he is  sort of his grip on the war is slipping  and he realizes that there are factions  of his own population who are starting  to protest obviously we saw the tv  protest last week and maybe he's just  trying to sort of show his own country  his own population that he is still in  control  interesting as well polly that it says  in the article this trust um  saying that the sanctions on russian  oligarchs are likely to be permanent  well i mean  what does permanent mean in this context  i mean i think what we would hope  is that russia can return to the family  of nations by potentially changing its  leader or most importantly changing its  approach  in a way it feels um  the the way that the oligarchs acquired  that wealth is through the kind of  complete messed up  voucher privatization uh as at the end  of the ussr and then the way that kind  of kleptocratic systems were set up to  to funnel power and wealth to those few  individuals  nevertheless you know i don't think what  we want is for us to be in a permanent  state of kind of a cold economic  conflict with russia of wanting to keep  russian businessmen and and russian  products out of out of europe out of  flowing into the rest of the world  but i think what liz trust is suggesting  is that it's very hard to imagine a  pathway back to being able to trust and  reintegrate russia when as she says you  know vladimir putin is is just creating  the illusion of these peace talks whilst  continuing  to  uh relentlessly bomb you know  almost obliterate certain towns in in  ukraine with no kind of sense that  actually what they're what they're  looking for is peace and it's therefore  very hard very hard to trust them  matt let's turn to the front page of the  daily telegraph of course the big power  broker here  china  we know that president biden was  speaking to  president xi  jinping uh earlier  today  where do we think this is going  well i think at the moment the western  western countries are really trying to  work out where china sits really in this  whole power play and um i think that the  next few days are going to be crucial um  to see how russia china really responds  to the to the war  and the telegraph reports um as as other  papers that china um is trying to work  out itself and china is a country  inherently reluctant to sort of comment  or interfere with other other nations um  affairs at least publicly and from its  politicians and obviously its actions  speak for something else but um  biden today and in his call was reported  to have warned that there'll be  implications and consequences of china's  if china does uh back russia's war and  aids it with military equipment and and  helps it dodge um  or circumvent uh economic sanctions um  what these implications and consequences  uh are who i mean is yet to obviously  seen but if if the west starts hitting  china with the same ferocity of economic  sanctions as it did with russia um i  think then that just hits and a  completely new level of um economic  impact for the whole world and and um i  think that's usually being warned that  this would be would have what  far-reaching consequences um for the  west as well uh and and  he was quoted as telling biden um that  he who tied the bell to the tiger must  take it off and i think that's quite a  worrying and dangerous kind of uh  quote there that um the war of words is  now starting to sound between the the  world's two biggest powers  yeah and and probably many people saying  that actually china's really  interested to see how all of this is  going to play out and concerns about  perhaps what china's intentions might be  towards the likes of taiwan for example  yeah i think it is  fascinating uh to watch china you know  the at the olympic games  uh there was this you know sort of  staged event between uh uh president xi  and president putin sort of coming  together to talk about an alliance to  talk about um the you know the the risks  of nato you know made a real play out of  being united together  and i think sen sort of shivers of of  worry across uh that nato alliance then  when russia took this extraordinary step  of just invading ukraine  china didn't immediately back them even  though as you say there are that they  have some sympathy for uh the sort of  the ethno-nationalist russian narrative  that ukraine really belongs to them  because there are some similarities to  the sense that there is a greater china  which includes taiwan  and tibet places that they believe  belong to them and ought to be kind of  fully incorporated into into the chinese  nation nevertheless they are still  kind of keeping their powder dry that  that quote matt used about  that it's sort of classic chinese  proverb isn't it about the bell on the  tiger is that sense that  this escalation has happened partly  because of nato partly because of you  know the warmth with which nato  expressed its openness to ukraine and  that sense which russia has absolutely  pushed that that it has a legitimate  sphere of influence uh a kind of  colonialist approach really to countries  around it that ought to just listen to  it and not face west um and that  therefore it's it is in the end the  west's fault of having aggravated putin  i think that's an extremely  damaging and dangerous narrative for any  of us to fall into in that you know  international relations are complicated  but invading a country is simply illegal  and wrong yeah indeed well i just want  to bring us on before we get before we  go to the break mat the other story  that's on a few of the front pages of  course uh perfect textbook example of  dreadful corporate pr isn't it the the  behavior of pno towards its workers  yeah and if it wasn't obviously for the  war in ukraine i think this would be the  front uh on the front of all papers uh  tomorrow um it's quite a shocking  situation 800 staff sacked within just  30 minutes notice on a pre-recorded zoom  call  and now the government is obviously  obviously facing a lot of pressure to  intervene and we hear tonight that uh  the business secretary has written  warning of um criminal sanctions against  the bosses and this now  but now we've hit  economic sanctions against so many  individuals in russia obviously that's  now raises the  sort of that  it leads to people to call for the same  scholar sanctions against these kind of  bosses who have just sacked so many  staff on the spot and um you can you can  see the trouble the government is going  to get itself in if it's uh it does  start uh pursuing pursuing that line of  um uh that the campaigners are calling  for yeah i think we're going to see a  lot more of that story next week aren't  we matt dathan polly mckenzie thank you  very much indeed

